00:00:06.923 --> 00:00:09.340
SPEAKER: I'm excited to have
Priyanka Vergadia, a customer

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:11.260
engineer at Google
Cloud, here with me

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:12.970
to show us how to
set up service level

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:14.363
monitoring in Stackdriver.

00:00:14.363 --> 00:00:14.905
Hi, Priyanka.

00:00:14.905 --> 00:00:15.760
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Hi, Stephanie.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:16.768
Thank you for having me.

00:00:16.768 --> 00:00:17.560
SPEAKER: Thank you.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:19.450
Google has published
our practices

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:22.330
for managing reliable
services at scale and site

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:25.900
reliability engineering
books and this SRE workbook.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.460
One of the main
principles of SRE

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.470
is using service
level objectives.

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:31.510
Can you show us how
developers can do the same?

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:32.140
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:34.400
Let's dive right in and let
me show it through a demo.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:34.960
SPEAKER: OK, great.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:36.220
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Perfect.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:42.530
So I have this website here that
is built to order some stuff.

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:45.610
And so I'm going to put
something in the cart.

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:49.000
It's an e-commerce website,
so it lets you order things,

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.390
and then it places an order.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:53.350
So I have my credit card
information in there,

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:54.910
and we just placed an order.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:57.610
Now, behind the scenes,
there is a lot going on

00:00:57.610 --> 00:00:59.840
in this particular application.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:02.000
So if you look at
what's going on here,

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.269
this is the service
architecture of the application.

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:06.360
And it has a front end.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.230
It has various
different services

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:10.060
talking to the front
end-- the check out,

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:13.300
the catalog, the product
catalog, the card service.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:14.990
There's a lot that's
going on here.

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:18.070
And if you see further,
they're pretty much all

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:21.290
written in very different
languages as well.

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:23.320
So there's a lot of
complexity behind the scenes

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:24.580
that's going on.

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:28.280
And this is all done through
microservices, as you can see.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:34.780
So it's important to understand
that a simple website can

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:37.510
be set up with different
services in the back end

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:39.310
and have different
languages that it can be

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:40.575
written in-- so complexities.

00:01:40.575 --> 00:01:41.200
SPEAKER: Right.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:42.760
And microservice
architectures mean

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:45.670
the app itself is broken
into many small pieces.

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:47.860
And doesn't that make
it hard to understand

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:49.720
how all the pieces
fit together and where

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:51.820
to start investigating
when there is a problem?

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:54.820
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: It becomes
very hard for the operations

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.010
team, especially in
microservices architecture,

00:01:57.010 --> 00:01:59.830
to figure out what's going
on, which services is up,

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:01.600
down, talking to each other.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:05.590
So there's a good tool in
Stackdriver Monitoring called

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:07.900
Topology, where you
can actually look

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:11.500
at the topology of your
different microservices

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:12.470
talking to each other.

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:16.840
So I have it open here, and when
you open the service topology,

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:20.020
I can look at a specific
service that I'm interested in,

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:22.740
and then see which other
services it is talking to.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.520
So it gives me a view.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:28.600
I can click on one, and it gives
me a view of what's going on

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:30.430
and which services
it is talking to,

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:32.380
which is pretty
helpful when you're

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.630
working in an
environment that has

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:37.703
so many different microservices
that it's talking to.

00:02:37.703 --> 00:02:39.370
SPEAKER: Wow, and
this is great because,

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:41.410
with microservice-based
apps, you can clearly

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:43.480
see the inter-service
dependencies

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:46.060
and use that to double-check
new code rollouts.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:48.580
But let's say you're
setting up alerts.

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:50.980
Ops staff can burn
out a lot of the times

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:54.370
from getting notified of all the
potential causes of a problem,

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:57.250
making it hard to align SREs
with customer interests.

00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:59.650
How can we identify which
behaviors really actually

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:01.870
matter for that service
during service degradation?

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:02.870
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:06.340
So there is also another tool
within Stackdriver Monitoring

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:11.950
for services, where you can
actually set up SLOs and define

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:16.030
your SLOs for the services so
operations does not actually

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:20.020
get affected by just looking at
all the different alerts that

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:20.800
can come by.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:24.250
It's actually a practice
within our SRE teams,

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:29.410
where we make sure that our
SRE staff is only monitoring

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:33.040
alerts that are related to
customer issues and front end

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:36.490
customer related issues and not
every single issue that might

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:39.540
be a cause to those issues.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:41.855
So let's see that
in the demo as well.

00:03:41.855 --> 00:03:42.480
SPEAKER: Great.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:43.480
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:47.030
So if we click on Services here
in the Stackdriver Monitoring,

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:48.860
let's look at front end.

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:53.660
And when we go do that, you're
able to see the service level

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:54.640
optimization.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.040
So let me click on SLOs.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:00.130
And in SLOs, you obviously
see nothing here.

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:03.430
So we're going to
set one up and say,

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:05.470
we want to set up
for availability,

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:08.890
and then we can do it
rolling or calendar.

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:15.070
I want to say, in the
month, if we reach 99%,

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:19.540
or low availability, then
I want to get alerted.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:21.370
That's a good way of
saying that you're not

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:22.910
going to be alerted
for everything

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:28.280
but you still want to be alerted
for when we go less than 99%.

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:32.590
And then you can also
set up a latency alert.

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:34.960
So in this case,
if we say latency--

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:37.510
and I may want to
set up differently

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:38.650
for different services.

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:41.110
For the front end,
you could say,

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:45.010
my pages should load in less
than 1,000 milliseconds,

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:47.500
otherwise I want to be notified.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:52.540
And in this case, also,
let's say it's 90% our goal,

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:53.660
and then we submit that.

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:55.618
So these are the two
alerts that we just set up

00:04:55.618 --> 00:04:58.420
for latency and availability.

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:00.610
And they are not just
thresholds because we

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:02.190
are saying this percentile.

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:04.780
So it's not going to
be chatty and noisy.

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:07.183
It's going to be,
if this happens,

00:05:07.183 --> 00:05:09.600
then we really want to get
notified because the service is

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:10.950
not working fine.

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:14.970
So that's a good way of
identifying, making sure

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:18.120
that you're not burning
out your operations staff.

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:19.180
SPEAKER: Excellent.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:21.630
How do you follow these best
practices given the resources

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:22.290
that you have?

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:23.290
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:25.960
So you also have metrics
and diagnostics in there.

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:28.020
So if you click
on Metrics, you're

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:31.620
able to see the request counts
and the response latencies,

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:34.503
which will guide you through
the request and response.

00:05:34.503 --> 00:05:36.420
And if you click on
Diagnostics, that actually

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:37.470
takes you to the Trace.

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:40.020
So now, if I want
to drill deeper

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:43.440
into the 95th percentile,
which is probably

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:46.020
the one I want to look at
because it's not performing

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:47.760
very well, then I
can click on that

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:52.507
and see a specific request, why
it was slow, for what reasons,

00:05:52.507 --> 00:05:54.090
and which requests
were actually slow.

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:56.820
So in this case, we can see
that the checkout service was

00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:59.040
a little bit slow
in send and receive,

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:00.870
so we may want to
delve deeper into that

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:02.530
and find out what's
going on there.

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:04.797
So the Diagnostics tab
and the Metrics tab

00:06:04.797 --> 00:06:06.630
are really good friends
for troubleshooting.

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:07.488
SPEAKER: Great.

00:06:07.488 --> 00:06:09.030
And, at some point,
you probably need

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:11.770
to dig a little deeper
into the services signals.

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:13.740
Let's say, maybe you
received an SLO alert

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:15.870
and there's no obvious
upstream cause,

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:18.630
or you have a customer complaint
outside of an SLO alert

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:20.550
that you need to
investigate, or maybe you

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:23.460
need to see how the new rollout
of your code version is going.

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:25.950
How do you get a coherent
display of all the signals

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:27.030
for a specific service?

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:29.040
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah,
so I think the best

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:31.020
thing to do at that point is--

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:33.240
you got an alert through SLO.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:35.700
You want to look at
metrics and diagnostics

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:37.472
to kind of figure
out what's going on

00:06:37.472 --> 00:06:39.180
because metrics and
diagnostics are going

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:42.510
to be helpful to understand
which specific service is not

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:43.350
performing well.

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:46.288
As you can see in diagnostics,
when we drilled deeper

00:06:46.288 --> 00:06:48.330
into a specific service,
we were able to find out

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:49.920
that checkout is the culprit.

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:51.590
And so we can drill
deeper into that.

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:54.840
So those metrics and diagnostics
would be your great friends

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:58.170
in order to find out
what's actually going on.

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:00.060
SPEAKER: So Stackdriver
service monitoring

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:02.143
gives you a whole new way
to view your application

00:07:02.143 --> 00:07:04.920
architecture, reason about
its customer-facing behaviors,

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:06.878
and get to the root of
any problem that arises.

00:07:06.878 --> 00:07:08.795
So thank you so much,
Priyanka, for showing us

00:07:08.795 --> 00:07:11.490
how Google is transforming the
ops experience of cloud native

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:12.450
and microservice deployments.

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:13.720
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Thank you for having me.

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:14.430
It was fun.

